WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.219
Here is a function that adds up amounts.

2
00:00:05.219 --> 00:00:08.495
It takes a MutableList of Number, and only reads it.

3
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.578
You have cents, a MutableList&lt;Int&gt;.

4
00:00:11.578 --> 00:00:13.141
An Int is a Number.

5
00:00:13.141 --> 00:00:15.525
So does total(cents) compile?

6
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:17.362
No.

7
00:00:17.362 --> 00:00:21.551
A MutableList&lt;Int&gt; is not a MutableList&lt;Number&gt;.

8
00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:23.901
A mutable list has two doors.

9
00:00:23.901 --> 00:00:25.741
get hands an element out.

10
00:00:25.741 --> 00:00:26.992
add takes one in.

11
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.881
If cents could pass, total could push 2.5 through the add door, into a list of ints.

12
00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:37.587
The element type sits at both doors, so MutableList stays invariant.

13
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.935
But total never uses the add door.

14
00:00:39.935 --> 00:00:44.896
So it asks for a view of the list with that door shut: MutableList&lt;out Number&gt;.

15
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.548
Read it as: a list of some subtype of Number, and I don't know which.

16
00:00:49.548 --> 00:00:51.739
Now cents fits, and so does rates.

17
00:00:51.739 --> 00:00:53.867
Every read comes out as a Number.

18
00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:56.473
Now total calls amounts.add(0).

19
00:00:56.473 --> 00:00:59.386
Zero is an Int, and cents holds ints.

20
00:00:59.386 --> 00:01:00.645
Does it compile?

21
00:01:02.833 --> 00:01:03.025
No.

22
00:01:03.025 --> 00:01:06.865
Through the view, the compiler can't tell which list it has.

23
00:01:06.865 --> 00:01:09.680
It could be rates, and zero is not a Double.

24
00:01:10.367 --> 00:01:12.834
javac calls that unknown type CAP#1.

25
00:01:12.834 --> 00:01:16.055
Nothing is known to fit it, so nothing goes in.

26
00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:19.564
Calls that take no element, like clear, still work.

27
00:01:19.564 --> 00:01:21.827
The view limits types, not changes.

28
00:01:22.033 --> 00:01:22.946
Now flip it.

29
00:01:22.946 --> 00:01:25.151
padWithZeros only adds zeros.

30
00:01:25.151 --> 00:01:29.029
So it asks for the other view: MutableList&lt;in Int&gt;.

31
00:01:29.233 --> 00:01:31.764
A list of Int, of Number or of Any.

32
00:01:31.764 --> 00:01:34.729
Each one takes an Int, so add(0) is safe.

33
00:01:35.033 --> 00:01:36.547
Reading gets weaker.

34
00:01:36.547 --> 00:01:43.132
The first element might be "EUR", so a read is typed only as Any?, which fits anything.

35
00:01:43.333 --> 00:01:47.481
Writing out or in on one use of a type is use-site variance.

36
00:01:47.481 --> 00:01:49.555
The view is a type projection.

37
00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:53.227
Java has only this kind of variance.

38
00:01:53.227 --> 00:01:57.443
? extends is its read view, and ? super its write view.

39
00:01:57.443 --> 00:02:01.047
Kotlin's out and in compile to these wildcards.

40
00:02:01.833 --> 00:02:02.647
The rule.

41
00:02:02.647 --> 00:02:06.893
A type that reads and writes T stays invariant.

42
00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:09.875
Only reads it: out, or ? extends.

43
00:02:09.875 --> 00:02:12.676
Only writes it: in, or ? super.

44
00:02:12.867 --> 00:02:13.710
Quick check.

45
00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:17.363
copyInto reads one mutable list and adds to another.

46
00:02:17.363 --> 00:02:20.033
Which one gets out, and which gets in?

47
00:02:22.433 --> 00:02:24.198
The from list gets out.

48
00:02:24.198 --> 00:02:25.732
The to list gets in.
